%% ACOS Capability Lifecycle
%% Architecture Chapter: 1.1 Enterprise Capability Architecture
%% Status: canonical
%%
%% This state machine defines the governed lifecycle for every registered
%% enterprise capability. A capability may only advance when the required
%% evidence, ownership, architecture, governance and operational controls are
%% complete. Transitions are auditable and fail closed.

stateDiagram-v2
  direction LR

  [*] --> Proposed

  Proposed --> ArchitectureReview: concept registered\nowner and purpose assigned
  Proposed --> Rejected: insufficient strategic fit

  ArchitectureReview --> Approved: architecture decision accepted\nADR linked\ndomain boundaries confirmed
  ArchitectureReview --> Proposed: revision required
  ArchitectureReview --> Rejected: violates constitution or architecture

  Approved --> Planned: roadmap priority and funding assigned
  Approved --> Deprecated: no longer strategically required

  Planned --> Implementing: implementation authorized\nacceptance criteria baselined
  Planned --> Approved: planning assumptions invalidated

  Implementing --> Validated: schema, registry, security and CI checks pass
  Implementing --> Planned: implementation reset or redesigned
  Implementing --> Deprecated: implementation abandoned by governance decision

  Validated --> Operational: release authorized\nrunbook, telemetry and rollback verified
  Validated --> Implementing: validation failure or material defect

  Operational --> Observed: production evidence accumulated\nSLOs and controls measured
  Operational --> RolledBack: material operational or governance failure
  Operational --> Deprecated: replacement or retirement approved

  Observed --> Improving: improvement hypothesis approved
  Observed --> Operational: no material change required
  Observed --> Deprecated: value, risk or duplication threshold breached

  Improving --> Validated: changed capability revalidated
  Improving --> Operational: non-material operational tuning completed
  Improving --> RolledBack: improvement introduces unacceptable risk

  RolledBack --> Implementing: corrective implementation authorized
  RolledBack --> Deprecated: recovery not viable or capability superseded

  Deprecated --> Retired: consumers migrated\ndata and obligations resolved\nretirement evidence complete
  Deprecated --> Improving: deprecation reversed by governance decision

  Retired --> [*]
  Rejected --> [*]

  note right of Proposed
    Required evidence:
    - unique capability ID
    - purpose and scope
    - business and technical owner
    - initial criticality
    - strategic rationale
  end note

  note right of ArchitectureReview
    Mandatory controls:
    - constitutional alignment
    - domain ownership review
    - dependency analysis
    - event and data boundary review
    - security classification
    - ADR linkage
  end note

  note right of Approved
    Approval means architecture acceptance,
    not permission for runtime side effects.
  end note

  note right of Planned
    Required planning artifacts:
    - delivery plan
    - budget and exposure allocation
    - acceptance criteria
    - rollout and rollback strategy
    - implementation references
  end note

  note right of Implementing
    Implementation remains non-operational
    until all validation gates pass.
  end note

  note right of Validated
    Validation gates:
    - registry schema validation
    - dependency resolution
    - cycle detection
    - policy traceability
    - security validation
    - test and CI evidence
    - implementation readiness review
  end note

  note right of Operational
    Operational entry criteria:
    - release authorization
    - observability coverage
    - runbook and ownership readiness
    - recovery objectives verified
    - audit correlation enabled
  end note

  note right of Observed
    Evidence reviewed:
    - business outcomes
    - SLO and reliability results
    - policy exceptions
    - security findings
    - human intervention rate
    - cost and exposure
  end note

  note right of Improving
    Every material change re-enters
    validation before operational use.
  end note

  note right of Deprecated
    Deprecation requires:
    - replacement or shutdown plan
    - consumer migration plan
    - data retention decision
    - policy and contract impact review
    - target retirement date
  end note

  note right of Retired
    Retirement is irreversible unless a
    new capability version is proposed.
    Historical audit evidence is retained.
  end note

  note left of RolledBack
    Rollback triggers include:
    - policy violation
    - material security risk
    - failed acceptance criteria
    - unacceptable business impact
    - loss of auditability
  end note
